---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';

const related = [
  { href: '/journal/brake-pad-sets/', label: 'What comes in a brake pad set?' },
  { href: '/journal/alternator-labels/', label: 'Reading the label on an alternator' },
  { href: '/library/', label: 'All resources' },
];
---

<Article
  title="When the vehicle record doesn’t match the job | Parts Relay"
  description="Resolve a discrepancy between the vehicle in the bay, its registration and the VIN on the repair order."
  heading="When the vehicle record doesn’t match the job"
  topic="Vehicle guide"
  topicHref="/library/"
  readTime="3 min read"
  relatedHeading="Once you’ve identified the vehicle"
  related={related}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    The car in the bay and the car on the screen should agree before you use a parts reference. A
    familiar model name can hide a different engine or variant, even within one registration year.
  </Fragment>
  <div
    slot="cover"
    class="lib-art lib-art-vin"
    aria-label="Illustrated vehicle identification plate with a barcode"
    role="img"
  >
    <div class="lib-plate">
      <span>Vehicle identification</span>
      <strong>VIN</strong>
      <i></i>
      <small>Vehicle identification number</small>
    </div>
  </div>
  <section>
    <h2>Where did the identifier come from?</h2>
    <p>
      Use the VIN on the vehicle or a verified shop record. If your supplier works from a
      registration plate, include the issuing market. A registration catalogue may cover some
      markets and leave others out.
    </p>
    <dl class="lib-record">
      <div>
        <dt>VIN</dt>
        <dd>
          Read back the characters in groups and check them against the vehicle. A format check can
          flag a typing error, even when the number looks familiar.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Registration</dt>
        <dd>Keep the plate with its issuing market when you ask for a lookup.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Model and engine</dt>
        <dd>
          Compare the description the supplier finds with the job sheet. Ask the technician about
          any discrepancy.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Equipment</dt>
        <dd>
          If more than one part fits the listed model, ask which option or marking distinguishes
          them.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Which details agree?</h2>
    <p>
      Read the make and model back against the repair order. Compare the engine or variant wherever
      the result includes it. If those details agree, open the relevant parts group. Leave any
      equipment the record doesn’t specify for the technician to confirm.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="lib-case">
    <h2>If the search returns an unexpected result</h2>
    <p>The next question depends on what the catalogue could identify.</p>
    <dl class="lib-evidence">
      <div>
        <dt>A different vehicle appears</dt>
        <dd>
          Check the identifier against the vehicle itself or the verified job sheet. Resolve the
          mismatch with the shop before you quote from that record.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The result is empty</dt>
        <dd>
          Confirm the registration and its issuing market. If the catalogue still has no entry, ask
          the supplier to identify the vehicle and record what the shop has verified so far.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The car matches, but the description is vague</dt>
        <dd>
          Ask where the part fits. “Bearing” could mean a front or rear wheel bearing, or a bearing
          inside another assembly. Keep the answer with that vehicle record.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <p>
    <a class="lib-inline-link" href="/tools/vin-check/">
      Check the format of a VIN
      <svg
        aria-hidden="true"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.55"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
      >
        <path d="M4 12h15m-6-6 6 6-6 6" />
      </svg>
    </a>
  </p>
  <p class="lib-product-note">
    Parts Relay finds the parts from a plate or VIN and keeps each search in one queue the whole
    counter can see.
    <a class="lib-inline-link" href="/about/">
      How Parts Relay works
    </a>
  </p>
</Article>
